it, including the effect of compounding. A higher compounding frequency gives a higher APY at the same interest rate.\"},{\"id\":\"beneficiary\",\"term\":\"Beneficiary\",\"definition\":\"For a retirement account, the person or entity named to receive its benefits after the owner dies. The account's beneficiary designation follows the plan's rules.\"},{\"id\":\"bitcoin\",\"term\":\"Bitcoin\",\"definition\":\"An open-source payment network that records transfers without a central operator. Bitcoin (BTC) is the digital asset used by the network.\"},{\"id\":\"broker\",\"term\":\"Broker\",\"definition\":\"For digital asset tax reporting, a business that carries out sales of digital assets for customers, such as a US crypto exchange. Brokers file Form 1099-DA.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"capital-gain\",\"term\":\"Capital gain or loss\",\"definition\":\"The difference between what you receive when you sell an asset and its cost basis. A positive difference is a gain and a negative one is a loss.\"},{\"id\":\"cash-flow\",\"term\":\"Cash flow\",\"definition\":\"Money that comes in minus money that goes out over a period. Transfers between your own accounts are neither.\"},{\"id\":\"cost-basis\",\"term\":\"Cost basis\",\"definition\":\"What you paid to acquire an asset, including fees and commissions. Your gain or loss on a sale is the proceeds minus the cost basis.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"covered-security\",\"term\":\"Covered security\",\"definition\":\"An asset whose cost basis the broker must report to the IRS. For digital assets, this generally means assets bought on or after January 1, 2026, in the same account at that broker.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"debt-to-income-ratio\",\"term\":\"Debt-to-income ratio\",\"definition\":\"Monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income. Lenders use it to judge whether a borrower can take on a new loan.\"},{\"id\":\"digital-asset\",\"term\":\"Digital asset\",\"definition\":\"The IRS term for a digital representation of value recorded on a cryptographically secured distributed ledger. It includes cryptocurrencies, stablecoins, and NFTs.\"},{\"id\":\"emergency-fund\",\"resources\":[\"/calculators/emergency-fund\"],\"term\":\"Emergency fund\",\"definition\":\"Cash set aside for unexpected costs or lost income. It is usually kept in an account you can withdraw from quickly, such as a savings account.\"},{\"id\":\"exchange\",\"term\":\"Exchange\",\"definition\":\"A company that holds customer assets and carries out trades between digital assets or between digital assets and dollars. A US exchange is a broker for Form 1099-DA reporting.\"},{\"id\":\"fifo\",\"term\":\"FIFO (first in, first out)\",\"definition\":\"A method that treats the earliest units you bought as the first units sold. The IRS applies it when you do not specifically identify the units you sold.\"},{\"id\":\"form-1099-da\",\"term\":\"Form 1099-DA\",\"definition\":\"The form US brokers send you and the IRS for sales of digital assets, starting with sales in 2025. For sales in 2026 and later, it also reports cost basis for covered assets.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"form-8949\",\"term\":\"Form 8949\",\"definition\":\"The IRS form where you list each sale of a capital asset with its dates, proceeds, cost basis, and gain or loss. Digital asset sales use boxes G through L.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"gas-fee\",\"term\":\"Gas fee\",\"definition\":\"The fee paid to a blockchain network to process a transaction. It is usually paid in the network's own token, such as ETH on Ethereum.\"},{\"id\":\"holding-period\",\"term\":\"Holding period\",\"definition\":\"The time between buying and selling an asset. It decides whether a gain or loss is short-term or long-term.\"},{\"id\":\"long-term-capital-gain\",\"term\":\"Long-term capital gain\",\"definition\":\"A gain on an asset held more than one year. It is generally taxed at 0%, 15%, or 20%, depending on taxable income.\"},{\"id\":\"net-worth\",\"resources\":[\"/calculators/net-worth\"],\"term\":\"Net worth\",\"definition\":\"The total value of what you own minus the total of what you owe. Assets include cash, investments, property, and digital assets; debts include loans and card balances.\"},{\"id\":\"noncovered-security\",\"term\":\"Noncovered security\",\"definition\":\"An asset whose cost basis the broker does not have to report, such as a coin you transferred in from a wallet or another exchange. The broker must report the proceeds and may choose to report the basis.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"proceeds\",\"term\":\"Proceeds\",\"definition\":\"The amount you receive when you sell or otherwise dispose of an asset. On Form 8949, proceeds go in column (d).\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"recurring-charge\",\"term\":\"Recurring charge\",\"definition\":\"A payment that repeats on a schedule, such as a subscription, rent, insurance premium, or loan payment.\"},{\"id\":\"schedule-d\",\"term\":\"Schedule D\",\"definition\":\"The IRS form that totals the gains and losses from Form 8949 and carries the result to Form 1040.\"},{\"id\":\"self-custody-wallet\",\"term\":\"Self-custody wallet\",\"definition\":\"A wallet where you hold the private keys, so no company holds the assets for you. A self-custody wallet has no broker, so its sales get no Form 1099-DA.\",\"guide\":\"/learn/taxes/1099-da-missing-basis\"},{\"id\":\"short-term-capital-gain\",\"term\":\"Short-term capital gain\",\"definition\":\"A gain on an asset held one year or less. It is taxed at the same rates as ordinary income.\"},{\"id\":\"specific-identification\",\"term\":\"Specific identification\",\"definition\":\"A method where you choose which units you sold, such as the units with the highest cost basis. You must identify the units no later than the time of the sale and keep records that show it.\"},{\"id\":\"stablecoin\",\"term\":\"Stablecoin\",\"definition\":\"A digital asset designed to keep a steady price, usually one US dollar. USDC and USDT are examples.\"},{\"id\":\"staking\",\"term\":\"Staking\",\"definition\":\"Committing a digital asset to help run a proof-of-stake blockchain in return for rewards.\"},{\"id\":\"staking-reward-income\",\"term\":\"Staking reward income\",\"definition\":\"Under IRS Revenue Ruling 2023-14, staking rewards are ordinary income in the year you gain control of them, at their fair market value then. That value becomes the cost basis of the rewards.\"},{\"id\":\"swap\",\"term\":\"Swap\",\"definition\":\"Trading one digital asset directly for another. For US taxes, a swap is a sale of the asset you give up.\"},{\"id\":\"tax-lot\",\"term\":\"Tax lot\",\"definition\":\"Units of an asset bought at the same time and price. Each lot has its own cost basis and holding period.\"},{\"id\":\"transfer-between-own-accounts\",\"term\":\"Transfer between your own accounts\",\"definition\":\"Money that moves from one of your accounts to another, such as checking to savings or an exchange to your bank. It is neither income nor spending, and moving a digital asset between your own wallets is not a sale.\"},{\"id\":\"wash-sale-rule\",\"term\":\"Wash sale rule\",\"definition\":\"A rule that disallows a loss on stock or securities if you buy substantially identical ones within 30 days before or after the sale. It applies to a digital asset only when the asset is also stock or securities for tax purposes, such as a tokenized security.\"}]}],\"$L19\",\"$L1a\",\"$L1b\"]}]}]\n8:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"c\",{\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L1d\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"error\":\"$undefined\",\"errorStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"errorScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L1e\",null,{}],\"templateStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"templateScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"notFound\":\"$undefined\",\"forbidden\":\"